// draft
// drafted
type Pool = { values: Text[] }
// drafted
type Row = { values: Text[] }

partial_product(iterables: Pool[], repeat: Int) -> Row[]
	note "starting from every pool's first value, advance the pools (the iterables listed in order, the whole list taken repeat times) one at a time from first to last, emitting a tuple after each step, so every value of every pool is emitted without the full product"
	# Every output tuple has exactly as many values as there are pools.
	all t in out: len(t.values) == len(iterables) * max(repeat, 0)
	# Each value in an output tuple is one of the values of the pool at that position.
	// reaching for: for every tuple t and every position p in t.values, the value at position p is
	// in the values of the pool at position p, where the pool list is iterables repeated
	// max(repeat, 0) times, i.e. iterables[p mod len(iterables)]; the notation has no way to bind
	// an element's position as an Int, no list repetition, and no %
	# The output is empty exactly when some pool is empty.
	len(out) == 0 <-> repeat > 0 and (any p in iterables: len(p.values) == 0)
	# When no pool is empty, the first tuple holds the first value of every pool.
	// reaching for: when no pool is empty, out[0].values equals the list of each pool's values[0],
	// taken over iterables repeated max(repeat, 0) times; the notation has no list repetition and
	// no way to build the list of first elements of a list of lists (no map over a slice per
	// element)
	# Each tuple after the first is the tuple before it with one position set to that pool's next
	# value and every other position kept.
	// reaching for: for every adjacent pair out[i], out[i+1] there is exactly one position p where
	// they may differ, out[i+1].values[p] is the element after out[i].values[p] in the values of
	// the pool at position p (iterables repeated max(repeat, 0) times), and every other position is
	// equal; the notation cannot range over adjacent pairs, index a list at a bound Int position,
	// or say "the next element after this occurrence"
	# The position that advances never moves back to an earlier pool from one tuple to the next.
	// reaching for: writing a(i) for the position advanced between out[i] and out[i+1], a(i) <=
	// a(i+1) for every i; the notation cannot name the advancing position as an Int or range over
	// consecutive pairs of tuples
	# Every value of every pool appears at that pool's position in some output tuple.
	// reaching for: for every pool position p (positions of iterables repeated max(repeat, 0)
	// times) and every value v in that pool's values, some tuple t in out has t.values[p] == v; the
	// notation cannot quantify over positions as Ints, index t.values at p, or repeat a list
	// example wanted: the docstring case, iterables "AB", "C", "DEF" at repeat 1: the single-value
	// middle pool never advances, so the advancing position goes straight from the first pool to
	// the third
	// example wanted: iterables "AB" at repeat 3: decided by the note's expansion into three pools
	// "AB", "AB", "AB", each advanced in turn
	// example wanted: no iterables at repeat 1: there are no pools, none of them is empty, so the
	// first-tuple sentence applies to a tuple with no values
	// example wanted: iterables "AB", "C" at repeat 0: repeating the list zero times leaves no
	// pools, the same case as no iterables
	// example wanted: iterables "AB" at repeat -1: a negative repeat leaves no pools, as Python's
	// tuple times a negative number gives the empty tuple
	// example wanted: iterables "AB", "", "C" at repeat 1: one empty pool, decided by the
	// empty-output sentence
	// example wanted: iterables "AB", "" at repeat 0: the empty iterable is never repeated into a
	// pool, so no pool is empty
	// example wanted: iterables "AA", "B" at repeat 1: advancing to the next value of the first
	// pool sets the same value again, so two consecutive tuples are equal
	// example wanted: iterables "ABC" at repeat 1: a single pool, so every tuple has one value and
	// the pool advances through all three
